@prompt
Hazel also has classic print-statement debugging. For some tasks we'll ask you to use these instead of probes. Click **Console** at the top of the Task Reference sidebar to switch to the print console. Now, when you have a `print(value);` in your code, it prints there when you click **Run**. Prints don't need strings, they can print any Hazel type polymorphically.

# Task
Add `print(daily);` inside `weekly_water` to log each daily watering amount, then watch the Console fill as the calls below run. There should be three new lines.

@hint
`print(daily);` before the result, inside the function.

@reference
### Print
```hazel
print(42);
"done"
```
`print` writes a value to the Console and continues. Each entry shows a sequence number and a source line.

@code
print("test");
print(2 + 2);
let weekly_water(daily: Int): Int =
  daily * 7
in
weekly_water(250);
weekly_water(180);
weekly_water(50)
